\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Anderson acceleration (Figure~\ref {fig:anderson_stiffness}) beats Picard fixed-point iteration on stiff contraction maps. Iteration ratio (Anderson / Picard) as a function of spectral radius $\rho $ at different dimensions ($d \in \{32, 128\}$). Anderson achieves sublinear cost advantage at $\rho \geq 0.99$, the regime where Picard becomes slow, relevant for solving implicit-depth models where spectral properties are tight.}}{20}{figure.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:anderson_stiffness}{{4}{20}{Anderson acceleration (Figure~\ref {fig:anderson_stiffness}) beats Picard fixed-point iteration on stiff contraction maps. Iteration ratio (Anderson / Picard) as a function of spectral radius $\rho $ at different dimensions ($d \in \{32, 128\}$). Anderson achieves sublinear cost advantage at $\rho \geq 0.99$, the regime where Picard becomes slow, relevant for solving implicit-depth models where spectral properties are tight}{figure.caption.18}{}}
