\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Council members measured under one harness invocation (\texttt  {lm\_eval}, bfloat16, \texttt  {limit} 200 for the ladder and 150 for the aligned set; single seed per model). MMLU is weighted over the 57 subjects by subject size. The five loglikelihood columns require no generation; GSM8K is generative and is reported under each model's own chat template with a 512-token budget and flexible answer extraction, without which it measures answer formatting rather than arithmetic. The oracle rows bound what selection could achieve: the per-subject oracle knows which player is best on each subject, and the per-example oracle knows which is right on each question. The gated figure is the realistic ceiling: an ungated oracle credits the council for answers its sole correct member holds with no confidence, which no rule can identify.}}{29}{table.caption.27}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ladder}{{6}{29}{Council members measured under one harness invocation (\texttt {lm\_eval}, bfloat16, \texttt {limit} 200 for the ladder and 150 for the aligned set; single seed per model). MMLU is weighted over the 57 subjects by subject size. The five loglikelihood columns require no generation; GSM8K is generative and is reported under each model's own chat template with a 512-token budget and flexible answer extraction, without which it measures answer formatting rather than arithmetic. The oracle rows bound what selection could achieve: the per-subject oracle knows which player is best on each subject, and the per-example oracle knows which is right on each question. The gated figure is the realistic ceiling: an ungated oracle credits the council for answers its sole correct member holds with no confidence, which no rule can identify}{table.caption.27}{}}
